Initial rebasing controls:
python -m pytest tests/test_conforming_lattice_rebasing.py -q --tb=short
46 passed in 64.46s before the additional contact-derivative cases and
additional Fourier-subset transformations below were added.
The four integer basis changes, applied to R(.31) diag(700,430), are:
[[1,3],[0,1]], [[1,0],[-2,1]], [[2,1],[1,1]], [[-1,2],[0,-1]].
All have determinant one. Both physical rectangle tiling directions are
covered. The controls include analytic material integrals in the original
cell origin, independent inverse-rule contact tensors, positive-gap energy
closures on exactly matched physical Fourier sets, mixed-axis contact
stacks through three engines, fifteen live JAX parameters checked against
two secant steps, and invalid eager/traced reduction or lattice inputs.

Contact-opening derivatives on equivalent physical Fourier sets:
python -m pytest tests/test_conforming_lattice_rebasing.py -q -k contact_opening_derivatives --tb=short
2 passed, 46 deselected in 187.70s.
Zero perpendicular and internal gap; independent inclusion loss with a
lossless background. Contact values match between lattice spellings within
3e-10 and the two opening derivatives within 3e-9 absolute. The original
contact continuation and residual guards are unchanged.

All four positive-gap Fourier-subset transformations:
python -m pytest tests/test_conforming_lattice_rebasing.py -q -k nonrectangular --tb=short
8 passed, 44 deselected in 2.47s.
Four of these cases overlap the initial run; four are additional. The
reference matches orders by their physical reciprocal vectors, captures
Q/G/Z from a larger orthogonal order envelope, and selects the exact subset
before closure. All five slots match within 5e-12 absolute.
The completed rebasing test file therefore contains 52 distinct cases.

